# Result and Discussion

The study demonstrates that ethical data privacy requires more than simply securing information against hackers. Organizations must also consider **why information is collected, whether the collection is necessary, whether individuals understand the processing, how long the information is retained, and whether automated decisions may create unfair outcomes**.

The college facial-recognition case demonstrates how a technology designed for a legitimate purpose can create additional privacy and ethical concerns if it is implemented without sufficient safeguards.

Responsible data handling requires a balance between organizational objectives and respect for individual privacy.

# Conclusion

Ethical considerations are an essential part of responsible data management. Privacy, informed consent, data minimization, surveillance, artificial intelligence, algorithmic bias, and the protection of vulnerable communities should all be considered before collecting or processing personal information.

Organizations should adopt **privacy by design**, collect only necessary data, provide clear information to individuals, protect personal information, limit retention, evaluate automated systems for bias, and maintain accountability for their data practices.

The ethical use of personal data is therefore not only about following legal requirements. It is also about respecting individual autonomy, fairness, transparency, and the reasonable expectations of people whose information is being processed.
